      "id": 184,
      "text": "gone. Completely irreversible. There is no customer service hotline to dispute the charge. Within seconds,"
    },
    {
      "id": 185,
      "text": "Alice's wallet, which is constantly listening to the chatter on the network, sees the transaction floating in the mempool. So her screen"
    },
    {
      "id": 186,
      "text": "lights up showing she received the funds. But the wallet displays the status as unconfirmed. Oh, because she has"
    },
    {
      "id": 187,
      "text": "the cryptographic proof that Joe sent it, but it isn't officially in the ledger yet. Exactly. And if we connect this to the bigger"
    },
    {
      "id": 188,
      "text": "picture. Back to the miners. Right. It brings us right back to the proof-of-work lotteries we were discussing earlier. The transaction is valid"
    },
    {
      "id": 189,
      "text": "but it hasn't been locked into the historical record. So Alice has to wait. Yep, Alice has to wait for a miner somewhere"
    },
    {
      "id": 190,
      "text": "in the world to win that 10-minute lottery, bundle Joe's transaction into a new block, and cement it onto the blockchain. And once"
    },
    {
      "id": 191,
      "text": "that winning miner solves the puzzle and broadcasts the new block, Alice's wallet updates the status to Confirmed. The money is officially cleared."
    },
    {
      "id": 192,
      "text": "No central bank required. No clearinghouse skimming a massive percentage. Just pure cryptography and decentralized"
    },
    {
      "id": 193,
      "text": "consensus. It's a remarkable journey of engineering, honestly. An entire global financial ecosystem operating strictly"
    },
    {
      "id": 194,
      "text": "on mathematics. It really is. We've covered incredible ground today. We really have. We started with the failures"
    },
    {
      "id": 195,
      "text": "of centralized digital cash in the 80s, which led to Satoshi Nakamoto solving the seemingly impossible Byzantine"
    },
    {
      "id": 196,
      "text": "Generals Problem. We looked at how miners secure the network through proof-of-work lotteries, adjusting difficulty on the fly. The"
    },
